## ✅ Primary Method: Using `Map` and `List`

### Example: Building a JSON string with `Map` and `List`

```java
import com.google.gson.Gson;

import java.util.*;

public class GsonExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a Map for the top-level JSON object
        Map<String, Object> root = new HashMap<>();

        // Add a simple key-value pair
        root.put("name", "John Doe");

        // Create a list of strings
        List<String> hobbies = Arrays.asList("reading", "coding", "gaming");
        root.put("hobbies", hobbies);

        // Create a nested map
        Map<String, Object> address = new HashMap<>();
        address.put("street", "Main St");
        address.put("city", "New York");
        root.put("address", address);

        // Create a Gson instance
        Gson gson = new Gson();

        // Convert the map to a JSON string
        String json = gson.toJson(root);

        // Print the result
        System.out.println(json);
    }
}
```

### Output:
```json
{
  "name": "John Doe",
  "hobbies": ["reading", "coding", "gaming"],
  "address": {
    "street": "Main St",
    "city": "New York"
  }
}
```

